/** YD001 full translations siman 119 */
export const TRANSLATIONS = {
  'baer-heitev': {
    '1#א': `To rely. Explanation: to buy from him; but they rely on him when he says regarding others that it is permitted, as the Mechaber wrote in seif 7. Shach:`,
    '1#ב': `Regarding prohibition. Shach wrote: some say other prohibitions that are literally of the Torah; but even wine — stam yeinam is only d'rabbanan, for they decreed upon it lest it be definite libation wine; and some say not only wine but even other matters where there is concern of prohibition that are entirely d'rabbanan, such as cheese and bread of gentiles in a place where they practice prohibition, and the like; and from the conclusion of the Rav's words it appears the same applies to milk — one does not buy from him unless he has a presumption of kosher status. And even though the Mechaber also wrote in siman 65 that one does not buy meat from any butcher who slaughters for himself and sells unless he had a presumption of kosher status — one must say a slaughtering butcher is different, for it is very common and the laws of slaughter are numerous and he can easily cause delay, pressing, or other disqualifications; and if he did not have a presumption of kosher status it is forbidden. But according to Rama in truth there is no distinction, and if one dines with him — the Rav's view is that even l'chatchila one may eat with him.`,
    '2#_': `Suspected. Shach wrote: he is not called suspected regarding that matter until he transgresses not for appetite — such as if forbidden and permitted were placed before him he would eat the forbidden; and some say regarding a habitual sinner who eats nevelah for appetite, it is forbidden to eat from his slaughter until he checks the knife for him, as written in siman 2, because knives are often nicked — therefore we are concerned he will transgress for appetite and not trouble to fix it. But here it deals with a matter where there is no concern he will now transgress for appetite; therefore he is not called suspected. Taz wrote: if he is so suspected that even when permitted food is before him he abandons it and eats forbidden — he is like a full gentile; therefore he wrote this Rama gloss has no explanation at all, see there. And Darkei Moshe wrote in the name of Hagahot Asheri that one suspected of a prohibition such that it is forbidden to eat with him — even permitted things, lest he feed him forbidden things as well, end of his words.`,
    '3#א': `Substitute. Shach wrote: only for selling he instructs permissibility, but to steal and substitute by hand he is not suspected. And that which appears in siman 118 seif 1 that one does not deposit with a suspected Israelite a d'oraisa forbidden item except with two seals and a d'rabbanan forbidden item with one seal — there it deals with one suspected to substitute, and they abbreviated there because I did not descend to explain there who is called suspected.`,
    '3#ב': `Ashamed. Shach wrote: from Rashba it appears where it is known she is not ashamed of the guest as is the way of an innkeeper today who cares nothing about the guest's food — it is permitted. But Tosafot explained the opposite — that she takes the good and gives the guest the bad because she toiled for him; accordingly where he pays her wages for trouble it is possible it is permitted, as Tosafot wrote regarding an agent: if one gives him wages he cannot impose an oath on him, for then he no longer instructs permissibility; and even for Rambam, from whose words it appears that even when one gives wages he can impose an oath because nevertheless he instructs permissibility lest he did not pay his wages properly — it is possible he concedes here regarding an innkeeper's wages.`,
    '4#_': `Others. Bach wrote: a butcher suspected regarding milk is suspected regarding all prohibitions — meaning even regarding a matter more severe than milk, see there, for a matter the public are not lenient about. Shach challenged him, for he learned this from Rashba's words, and there it only appears regarding one suspected for a matter the public are not lenient about — he is suspected for that matter to substitute, but not for another matter.`,
    '5#_': `They are careful. Shach wrote: perhaps the Mechaber's view is that everything depends on what people are careful about; but where in people's eyes both are equal, we follow the lighter in penalty and the severe in penalty — requires study. Nevertheless all this only deals with one suspected regarding a severe matter, whether in penalty or in people's eyes, who is suspected regarding a lighter matter than it if they are one type of prohibition, such as both being food matters and the like; if not so, no (for a habitual sinner for one matter is not a habitual sinner for the entire Torah except Shabbat and idolatry — see Taz). And so from the Mechaber's words in siman 2 who wrote that a habitual sinner regarding circumcision is not a habitual sinner regarding slaughter; and in the Perishah he wrote he is not suspected regarding a lighter matter unless he has benefit from it — Shach wrote on this that this is not compelling, see there.`,
    '6#_': `Both of them. And it appears that even regarding two types of prohibitions, if he returned and was suspected afterward regarding one of them — even the lighter — he is suspected regarding the more severe because he was suspected initially regarding both. Shach.`,
    '7#א': `Regarding idolatry. Shach wrote: know that a habitual sinner for libation of wine is also a habitual sinner for the entire Torah.`,
    '7#ב': `In other matters. Even regarding his own, even to sell, and likewise to eat with him — only regarding prohibition we say so; but regarding monetary matters, one suspected regarding one matter is disqualified to testify regarding another matter even regarding others', as written in Choshen Mishpat siman 34. And some say that here and in siman 124 seif 9 the Rav ruled anonymously like the Mechaber; and in Choshen Mishpat siman 34 s.k. 205 and in Even HaEzer end of seif 42 the Rav wrote that one suspected regarding sexual immorality is disqualified for testimony about a woman — there the reason is he has an interest in the testimony, for it is pleasing to him that she be available to him frequently, or that she not be available and stolen waters are sweet, etc.; see above siman 127 in the gloss. However all this is regarding a suspected person; but regarding a habitual sinner, meaning one who is established — I wrote above siman 2 and below siman 127 that he is not believed regarding that matter regarding others', end of his words Shach.`,
    '7#ג': `Believed. Shach wrote: one may say this is what the Rav meant: one suspected regarding a matter that does not appear to people as a transgression is not called suspected that he should not be believed even under oath; however regarding that matter he is not believed without an oath — and so from the Rav's responsum in siman 124, see there.`,
    '7#ד': `Others. Shach wrote: certainly "before the blind do not place a stumbling block" he did not transgress; and this is specifically when others know he practices prohibition in the matter. And some say: behold the Rav wrote in siman 112 seif 15 that one who is careful with gentile bread and eats with others who are not careful — he may eat with them because of hatred; and likewise he may eat with them, for it is different there because bread is the main part of the meal — they permitted him because of hatred since its prohibition is not stringent, as the Rav wrote there. In the responsum of Maharar Levi ibn Chaviv siman 121 he greatly elaborated these laws, and I saw fit to clarify briefly the content and distinctions emerging from his responsum: one who practices prohibition in some matter because he holds the law is so and eats in the house of one who permits it — if the prohibition is recognizable to the one who forbids, the feeder may feed him, for if he did not hold so he would not eat; but when the prohibition is not recognizable it is forbidden, and forbidden to cook for him in his vessels even if they are not ben yomo; and all the more forbidden for the forbearer to tell the permitter to cook for him in vessels that are not ben yomo; but when the permitter cooks l'chatchila for him and for others' need it is also permitted, for it is b'dieved. One who forbids a matter because of ancestral custom and people of his province who practice so — forbidden for the permitter to feed him even if the prohibition is recognizable, just as it is forbidden d'rabbanan to hand a cup of wine to a nazir even though the nazir could take it himself, see above siman 151 seif 1. The forbearer may cook l'chatchila in the permitter's vessels that are not ben yomo or in plain vessels, for we also establish they are not ben yomo since they did not accept the custom to be so stringent; and if the vessel owner is before us we must ask him if he is ben yomo or not; and if the vessel owner is suspected we need not ask; but if he said so himself or they asked and he answered, we rely on him whether ben yomo or not, see above siman 122. All this regards the vessel; but the forbearer is forbidden to eat from the permitter's cooking if he is suspected based on his words that there is no prohibition in it, if the dish is from something customarily containing that fat; but if he is not certainly suspected, only a plain ignoramus — the forbearer may lodge with him even if he is not wise and expert in laws, if he knows the eater forbids that food, whether because of custom or because he holds the law is so. All this is in general; but if there is a custom they act according to their custom — such as in Rhine provinces some places practice prohibition on stomach-milk, and when they come to places that practice leniency they eat from their cooking from the sauce or from meat cooked with the prohibition, and all the more they use their vessels which are ben yomo; specifically when the custom is in food, such as those places practicing prohibition being in the same province that practices leniency and their custom is to eat from their cooking; but if the custom is only in the feeder alone it is not a custom; therefore one from Poland or the like without that custom, when he comes to places practicing leniency — even though they feed neighboring places that practice prohibition — it is forbidden for him to eat from their cooking. However that which it is forbidden to eat something forbidden because of ancestral custom and people of his province — specifically when he does not see leniency in the matter; but when he sees leniency in the matter and comes from a place that practiced prohibition to a place that practiced leniency, he may eat; and it appears even if he intends to return to a place that practices prohibition, since it appears to him it is permitted, see above end of seif R' Yehuda David, end of his words.`,
    '8#_': `Under oath. The reason: since he is already suspected regarding the oath to transgress, for he is under oath from Sinai; and even though the oath from Sinai is for a past transgression, since he is under oath and also transgressed a sin he is worse still; and further there is no distinction between a future oath or a past oath except regarding monetary matters, but not regarding prohibition — see Choshen Mishpat siman 92 seif 2 and siman 87 seif 15. And it appears all the more he is not believed regarding a lighter matter than it under oath. Maharshal wrote: nevertheless one suspected regarding stam yeinam as in these generations may be believed regarding yayin nesech when he swears on it, only they should specify the oath well that he is not under oath from Sinai, because he is lenient regarding stam yeinam which is d'rabbanan, end of his words; and he wrote he so ruled in practice; and perhaps he did not rule so except regarding stam yeinam because people are lenient about it, but one should not rule so regarding other d'rabbanan prohibitions; and even regarding cheese one should not believe one suspected about it even under oath, for even regarding d'rabbanan prohibitions one may say he is under oath from Sinai for the prohibition "do not turn aside," end of his words Shach.`,
    '11#_': `Libation. Shach wrote: the matter is plain that for all prohibitions he is not believed; only he does not make yayin nesech since he is truly a Jew; but regarding reliability in prohibitions, since in any case he worships idols for desire, plainly he is not believed.`,
    '13#א': `The money. Because of penalty — Rashi; and accordingly it appears specifically when it is known he knew they were forbidden things and sold them intentionally we penalize him; if not, no, for perhaps he was inadvertent (and one does not penalize for what he already ate — only that he need not pay except the value of tereifah cheaply; but what remains intact is mistaken transaction and he returns it and the buyer returns the money). And even though explained in seif 17 that a butcher has no excuse to say he was inadvertent — that is regarding prohibition but not to extract money, end of his words Shach. And Taz wrote it appears that even if the prohibition is safek tereifah — what he ate he ate and he returns the money and they did not consider his eating as benefit at all (and Nachalat Binyamin disagrees, for in safek tereifah even if the seller knew it was safek tereifah he does not return money when eaten; and so in Semak Choshen Mishpat siman 234; and what Taz brings from Bechorot is not proof, for there regarding prohibitions of benefit specifically we say so, or regarding a firstborn because he transgressed a rabbinic enactment, see there).`,
    '13#ב': `He shall pay. Since he did not cause him prohibition. Rashi.`,
    '13#ג': `Returns. The reason: Semak wrote in Choshen Mishpat that siman regarding d'rabbanan prohibition his eating is considered benefit; and Shach wrote the reason: regarding money they established it on Torah law and did not decree prohibition in order to extract money, end of his words.`,
    '14#א': `Scribes. And that which is stated in siman 39 seif 2 that if the lung was not checked it is kosher — that is only if it happened once through coercion or inadvertence; but that one who does not know how to check stands and checks intentionally and they ate based on his checking — certainly forbidden by law; and even though this checking is only of their words, for it is like the case of not nullifying prohibition l'chatchila — if they nullified intentionally it is forbidden; and so the Mechaber in siman 99 seif 6, end of his words Shach. And Taz wrote: and even though regarding a butcher we say in Choshen Mishpat siman 306 he is exempt to pay in this law that it is forbidden because of doubt — it is different there because you come to obligate him for his act; therefore one only obligates him for certainty and not doubt; unlike here between seller and buyer, even if the matter is doubt it is mistaken transaction and he would not have given money accordingly — therefore he returns his money. And in Beit Yosef he wrote in the name of Achai: an incident occurred with a needle found in the lung inside the meat after cutting and they forbade the animal, and what he sold and received money he does not return, because the prohibition is not certain but only because of doubt they forbade the animal since it cannot be checked by blowing, and regarding money the burden of proof is on the claimant, end of his words. Behold they compared this law to the butcher case I mentioned, and this is certainly not according to what I wrote above that even in doubt he must return money; nevertheless they judged well, for perhaps it deals with when they ate the meat before it became known it was tereifah, and in this there is no penalty to return money; or even if not yet eaten, perhaps the needle was swallowed before the buyer bought it (meaning regarding the seller who sold the whole animal to the butcher he returns money — investigate) lest it swallowed afterward, as Choshen Mishpat siman 232 seif 11: if within three days he bought, the claimant must bring proof; but if clear it was done with the seller's permission, such as after slaughter he sold to him — clearly mistaken transaction and he returns his money as long as he did not eat the meat; and if he ate, the selling butcher is exempt even though doubt d'oraisa prohibition — nevertheless no penalty applies since he himself did not know; unlike mistaken transaction, as long as meat remains it does not help him that he did not know, for whatever is because of a defect he must return even though he too benefited — plain in Choshen Mishpat siman 232 seif 18 in the gloss. But in truth if the seller knew this doubt and sold to him — certainly even if the buyer ate he returns his money, unlike Levush, see there, end of his words.`,
    '14#ב': `From an animal. Taz wrote: even though Shulchan Aruch wrote that if tereifah came because the lung was not checked properly we say if she ate the seller deducts and does not return — nevertheless that is only regarding something that d'oraisa does not require checking when there is no defect; but if there is a defect it automatically requires checking even d'oraisa, and the event occurred that they cannot check — even if the buyer ate the seller must return, for behold he sold doubt d'oraisa prohibition; and this is specifically if the seller knew the doubt, for then penalty applies; if he did not know, he does not return except when not eaten, from mistaken transaction. But Shach disagrees and wrote: since it is doubt, why should the seller return money if already eaten; if the buyer wants to say you have no greater defect than this, the seller says return my sale and I will return your money; now that he ate the seller says who can tell you that you ate tereifah — and so in Semak Choshen Mishpat siman 234 s.k. 1 regarding safek tereifah if eaten he need not return money; rather this is the explanation of Rama's words here: from an animal not checked properly which is certainly forbidden by their words — plainly he must return money while meat exists; but even if he sold something from an animal in which a defect arose requiring forbidding out of doubt, and the seller might say perhaps it is not tereifah and he will bring proof it is tereifah and return money — nevertheless it is as if certainly tereifah by their words and he must return money while meat still exists; and any safek tereifot voids the transaction if meat still exists; but if he already ate the meat the seller need not return anything, end of his words.`,
    '15#_': `He shall go. Maharshal wrote: if the sages see he cannot exile from his place because dependents hang on him and his daughter's troubles or the like — they give him repentance in his place with enough restriction to stand in this balance. He also wrote that for one transgression they consider him a stumbling block and should warn him not to do so again. And nevertheless it appears one should forbid vessels retroactively whenever there is doubt even the first time. Shach wrote that even the first time he sold forbidden things they remove him, etc.; and so from the Mechaber seif 17 and what the Rav wrote in Choshen Mishpat siman 306 seif 9: and some say a butcher and teacher even though they do not need warning, nevertheless we require presumption until they are established or permitted — we do not remove them. Ribash already answered: there it deals with only monetary loss and also perhaps they are misled or inadvertent; but one suspected of feeding prohibition — immediately they remove him. Rashba already wrote that a butcher from whom tereifah issued has no excuse to say he was inadvertent, for otherwise he would always say so; and regarding tereifah of vessels see above siman 1. Beit Yosef wrote in Choshen Mishpat siman 34: according to Rambam, if he inspects for himself, since he has monetary benefit, even if he repented he is not restored until he goes where he is not recognized; one who inspects for others, since he has no monetary benefit therein, is restored through repentance.`,
    '16#_': `From paying. Since it is only safek tereifot he need not pay the animal's value to the owners, for he can say perhaps it is kosher, and the burden of proof is on the claimant; see Choshen Mishpat siman 306 seif 5. Shach.`,
    '17#_': `Excuse. However regarding a person known to be God-fearing and meticulous in his actions — in Mahari Weil's responsum they do not remove him and we say he certainly erred, see there.`,
    '19#א': `So-and-so. The reason: he fears lest they ask that so-and-so and he will not lie; and it appears even when the sender did not instruct him to buy from expert so-and-so but he himself says I bought from expert so-and-so — he is believed. Shach.`,
    '19#ב': `To substitute. And if you fear perhaps he gives from his own and a person is not apt to give a gift from his own in another's name; and even if he does not mention the expert's name but says a certain expert sent you a gift — he is believed. Shach.`,
    '19#ג': `D'oraisa. Taz disagrees with the Rav, for in siman 2 seif 4 regarding a habitual sinner for appetite who said expert so-and-so slaughtered for me — he is believed, and there it is slaughter which is d'oraisa prohibition and nevertheless believed; and another proof from siman 86 we rely on the gentile when he says these eggs are from such-and-so bird, and behold d'oraisa prohibition; and also siman 118 regarding sending a thigh by a gentile cut in the way they cut to remove the gid, etc. — even d'oraisa we apply this reasoning that one does not give from his own in others' names, see there. But Shach answers these difficulties: in siman 2 it deals when we know clearly he did not slaughter — no concern, for most available at slaughter are experts, unlike here; and what we rely on in siman 86 regarding eggs — one may say there since he himself sells to him; but here when he says he bought from another he is not believed in d'oraisa prohibition. However what Taz challenged from siman 118 regarding thigh — Shach did not address this; requires study for practical halachah whether we say this reasoning regarding d'oraisa prohibition.`,
    '20#_': `Suspected. Shach wrote the reason: because he is a habitual sinner for that matter but not for other matters — plainly if suspected for one matter he is not suspected for other matters, unlike Bach, see there. And Taz wrote Beit Yosef brought here in the name of Rambam that a suspected person and all the more a gentile is believed regarding others' property to testify that he tithed, for a person does not sin without benefit to himself; and he disagrees, for we establish one suspected regarding a matter is neither judged nor testifies, and so one should rule in practice, end of his words.`,
  },
  'beer-hagolah': {
    '1#א': `Mishnah Maasrot chapter 2 mishnah 2:`,
    '1#ב': `Regarding the law of milk and cheese mentioned in the baraita below:`,
    '2#א': `Baraita Avodah Zarah daf 39:`,
    '2#ב': `There it supports Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi, etc.`,
    '3#א': `Baraita Chullin daf 6:`,
    '3#ב': `Mishnah Demai chapter 3 mishnah 6, and they bring it there in the Gemara:`,
    '3#ג': `There in mishnah 5 and there in the Gemara:`,
    '4#א': `Mishnah there Bechorot daf 29, and according to R' Shimon:`,
    '4#ב': `There regarding the gentile butcher, etc.`,
    '5#א': `Mishnah there daf 30:`,
    '5#ב': `Statement of Rabbi Yochanan there according to the Sages:`,
    '6#_': `Resolution of Rabbi Yannai there on the mishnah that one may be suspected regarding purity matters:`,
    '7#_': `Rashba in a responsum:`,
    '8#_': `Responsa of Ramban 170, and see above siman 2 seif 3:`,
    '9#_': `Rashba in a responsum:`,
    '10#_': `There:`,
    '11#_': `There in the name of R' Yonah who heard from the sages of France:`,
    '12#_': `Ribash in a responsum (and see above siman 129 seif 9):`,
    '13#א': `Mishnah Bechorot daf 37:`,
    '13#ב': `Rambam in chapter 16 of Laws of Sales (and the Mechaber repeated it in Choshen Mishpat siman 234) and Ribash wrote he learned this from that the mishnah mentioned only d'oraisa prohibitions; but a d'rabbanan prohibition — since he ate and benefited he does not return money:`,
    '14#_': `Responsum of Ribash there:`,
    '15#_': `Statement of Rav Idi bar Avin, Sanhedrin daf 25:`,
    '16#_': `Ribash in a responsum:`,
    '17#_': `Rashba in a responsum:`,
    '18#_': `Rosh in a responsum:`,
    '19#_': `Tur in the name of Rashba from mishnah in first chapter of Demai daf 16:`,
    '20#א': `Rashba in Teshuvot HaRosh and I cited it above in siman in seif 8:`,
    '20#ב': `There Rashba, and it appears to me a printing error fell in and this "some say" should be at the beginning of the seif; likewise above in that siman and seif this law in the name of "some say":`,
  },
  'beur-hagra': {
    '1#א': `One suspected, etc. Bechorot 30a and other places.`,
    '1#ב': `Whether, etc. Chapter 2 of Demai — he said regarding himself, etc.; and there in several places; and Baer Heitev.`,
    '1#ג': `And if one dined with him, etc. Chapter 2 of Demai as above. (Lekut) And if one dined with him, etc.; and even though we say in Avodah Zarah 39b, etc., if one dined, etc. — Rashi there s.v. one does not buy, etc., as written in seif 2 (until here).`,
    '1#ד': `And some say, etc. Avodah Zarah 39b — baraita: one does not, etc.; and Rambam explained it deals with plain people we do not recognize; he mentioned Syria and all the more outside the Land; excluding Eretz Yisrael when majority of Israel was upon it; and now Eretz Yisrael is like outside the Land for this; and the first reasoning followed Rashi there s.v. one does not buy, etc.; and see seif 2.`,
    '1#ה': `However if, etc. Gemara there.`,
    '2#_': `Transgressing a sin, etc. Taz challenged this (and see below s.k. 18).`,
    '3#א': `Since he is not, etc. There: now he steals a theft, etc.`,
    '3#ב': `And if, etc. As written above.`,
    '3#ג': `Sometimes, etc. As Rashi explained there.`,
    '3#ד': `Nevertheless, etc. As written in chapter 3 of Demai regarding deposit, etc.; and Gittin 61b — they grind and deposit, etc.; and there it appears equivalent to giving to prepare as written because he wants to substitute, etc.; and it appears this was before the answer there as the mishnah states the reason, etc.; and one might think they are suspected to steal and substitute; but according to the answer — because he wants specifically when giving to prepare, but regarding deposit the desire does not apply as written, and he returns the items, etc.`,
    '4#א': `One suspected, etc. Baer Heitev; ruled like R' Shimon because the Gemara there follows him. (Lekut) One suspected for one matter, etc. — as anonymous mishnah Bechorot 30a: one suspected regarding, etc.; and even though R' Meir disagrees as written — some say Rabba bar bar Chana, etc.; halachah follows the Sages and anonymous mishnah; and Tosafot there 36a s.v. perhaps, etc.; and so we establish in Chullin s.v. let us say supports, etc. (until here).`,
    '4#ב': `(Lekut) But everything needed, etc. Like R' Shimon — Rava acted there accordingly (until here).`,
    '5#א': `One suspected, etc. Gemara there — why Sabbatical Year, etc.; the whole discussion there.`,
    '5#ב': `Unless. There in R' Yehudah's locale, etc.`,
    '7#א': `One who, etc. There — not like R' Meir; likewise first chapter of Chullin.`,
    '7#ב': `Except idolatry, etc. There.`,
    '7#ג': `(Lekut) Or does not believe, etc. Like a Samaritan — not only regarding others' property is he not believed, for "before the blind do not place a stumbling block," as first chapter of Chullin (4a) and explicit in Niddah (57a); but even regarding his own it is forbidden, for we establish like R' Eliezer as in Gittin and as above siman 2 seif 9; all the more he is not believed regarding his own because of "before the blind" as above (until here).`,
    '7#ד': `And regarding others' property he is believed even regarding that matter to say it is permitted — only regarding prohibition we say so; but regarding monetary matters one suspected regarding one matter is disqualified to testify regarding another even regarding others', as Choshen Mishpat siman 34. And some say here and siman 124 seif 9 the Rav ruled like the Mechaber; Choshen Mishpat 34:205 and Even HaEzer end seif 42 — Rav wrote one suspected regarding sexual immorality is disqualified for testimony about a woman; there the reason is interest in testimony. Difficult: we decide halachah end Yoma and Bechorot 39b like Rashbag against R' Meir; Rashba 64 and Ribash 4 answered anonymous mishnah then dispute; Raavad: Rashbag disputes R' Meir only on blemishes; Demai ch.4 after dark — Yerushalmi Shabbat honor; entering city proves guest leniency; Rambam only when unrecognized; Rashbag believed on another's property; plain person per Rambam not trusted but believed on others unlike suspected; prohibitions even woman and slave believed siman 118:8; Rashba 64, 400; difficult Rama on appetite — see siman 2 s.k. 12.`,
    '7#ה': `One who is suspected, etc. As written first chapter Bava Metzia (5b) "do not covet" for people; chapter Zeh Borer Sanhedrin 26b regarding those buried, etc.`,
    '7#ו': `However, etc. Since he thinks it permitted — practical difference whether called suspected so believed under oath — suspected not believed as seif 8; as beginning Bava Metzia and "do not covet" — Rama and Shach responsa.`,
    '7#ז': `One who practices, etc. As Yevamot 14 — he informed them; chapter 25 (111b) he cares for ancestral seed, etc.`,
    '8#_': `One suspected, etc. As Bava Metzia 6a conclusion: presumption suspected for money is suspected for that matter on oath — except old loan doubt; unlike here. Chullin 4: only helps checking knife; Bechorot end accepting fellowship — they did not say oath helps; Rashba 64.`,
    '9#_': `Convert, etc. Sanhedrin 61b — one who worships idolatry, etc.; we establish like Rava exempt; Tosafot there; Shabbat 72b Rava; Rambam Sanhedrin 20:2; exempt from lashes as Rambam — "to the girl you shall do nothing" — all coercion from fear of death is coercion as Rambam and Ketubot 3b modest women, etc.; Rava exempt completely and karet as Torat Kohanim that one — not coercion; since court does not punish, not disqualified for testimony Rambam and Shulchan Aruch Choshen Mishpat 34:2; even d'rabbanan disqualification without proclamation as chapter 7 and Gittin 45b convert returned, etc.; as above siman 401 seif 2 and Orach Chaim 39:3.`,
    '10#_': `Informer, etc. As above siman 2 seif 9; disqualified for testimony but believed as above.`,
    '11#_': `Apostate, etc. As Shach — he acts from love and exempt as seif 9.`,
    '12#_': `Forced ones. As seif 9; Ribash 4 and 11.`,
    '13#א': `One who sells, etc. It appears the reason he is not in commerce — though if bought surplus he may sell, primary purpose is not trade; and if sold to gentile or dogs as there; see there.`,
    '13#ב': `And all benefit-prohibitions, etc. Mishnah there yayin nesech and Gemara there even Rashba what did I lose you; and no law of sale.`,
    '14#א': `One who sells, etc. As Yerushalmi chapter 3 Yevamot — R' Yudan asked what proof from their words for tereifah sight, etc.; see siman 39 seif 1–2.`,
    '14#ב': `And if he sold from an animal, etc. Meaning seller cannot say bring proof it is tereifah — as muggeret in Bava Kama 99b: since safek tereifah no greater defect — mistaken transaction as if certainly tereifah; unlike animal not checked standing on presumption of permission Chullin 9a; unlike firstborn — Rashi Bechorot s.v. he returns, etc.`,
    '15#_': `Without subterfuge. Since lest he deceive, etc.; rather, etc.`,
    '16#א': `Butcher whose actions, etc. As first chapter Chullin (18a) that butcher, etc., we remove, etc.; Hagahot ShaDa (93b) and halachah to flog him, etc.`,
    '16#ב': `Nevertheless, etc. As muggeret as above.`,
    '17#_': `Butcher, etc. Otherwise how disqualify all disqualifications who could say so.`,
    '18#א': `Butcher, etc. As Choshen Mishpat 34:24 — but if they saw him, etc.; see there.`,
    '18#ב': `And that which he must, etc. As end Bechorot — baraita: one who comes, etc.; need not act like butcher — many are lenient there not wicked intending to mislead unlike here; transgressed and Rashi Gemara 41a and all, etc.`,
    '19#א': `(Lekut) From expert so-and-so, etc. Even though sender did not instruct buy only from expert — unlike Raah who said only like mishnah told buy from so-and-so; truth mishnah Bechorot 36b supports; but we do not hold so — rather Rav there: priest believed, etc. (until here).`,
    '19#ב': `He brought him a gift, etc. Bechorot 36a — R' Yochanan believed, etc.; see sugya; and Rashba first chapter Chullin.`,
    '19#ג': `In what case, etc. As first chapter Chullin (6b); end Nezikin — now he steals a theft, etc.`,
    '19#ד': `And all this does not, etc. We only find this in Demai — Beit Yosef; not so — we say chapter Eggs (63b) buying eggs when he says, etc.; even gentile per Rav siman 86 because of exposure; all the more sending where he fears as Bechorot end; certainly supports; we are not concerned to substitute even one suspected d'oraisa Chullin 6a giving to neighbor's wife, etc.; not because of Shevi'it — Tosafot suspected on Shevi'it; see siman 118 seif 8; siman 2 seif 4; Taz and Peri Chadash challenged.`,
    '20#_': `One suspected, etc. And some say, etc. Baer Heitev s.k. 28; as chapter 2 Avodah Zarah chavit — Rashi and R' Yehudah explained suspected only substitutes for that matter; Shach.`,
  },
  'kaf-hachayim': {
    '1#א': `(1) [seif 1] One suspected of eating forbidden things. A great principle: all laws of suspected persons in this siman do not require testimony before court that the man transgressed — then he would be disqualified for testimony too; even rumor suffices — an incessant rumor makes him suspected. Arukh HaShulchan s.k. 20; similarly Chakrei Lev part 1 Yoreh Deah (167): clear testimony not required — even estimation or rumor suffices. Most common people tithe yet were suspected on tithes because the minority causes suspicion — same in other matters — Rashba (64, 400), Ribash (213), Maharik (root 33), Rashdam (41), other Acharonim; see Chakrei Lev (193); Chayei Adam (1); see responsa Maaseh Ish (13); Zivchei Tzedek below s.k. 116; responsa Parashat Mordechai (7); Devarim Chaim Minsanz (4); Ramatz (12–13); Levushei Mordechai (8). However where enemies spread rumor he is not suspected — Arukh HaShulchan; Maharash Engel (17).`,
    '1#ב': `(1) I saw in Yad Yitzchak part 1 (5) that one is not called suspected unless an incessant rumor that he did this prohibition many times and he is unrestrained; similarly Pnei Yehoshua part 1 (16). I disagree: any suspicion he did prohibition even once — not believed; Shach below (18): suspected on prohibition not believed on it but believed on others'; habitual sinner not believed even on others'; Kenesset HaGedolah; Beli; Chokhmat Adam; Zivchei Tzedek.`,
    '1#ג': `(1) Even though generally a person does not make himself wicked — if he spread rumor on himself that he transgressed or sold prohibition to others he is suspected; Choshen Mishpat 34:25, 92:5 — not made witness ab initio; likewise for prohibition reliability — not one witness on prohibitions l'chatchila; Hashiv Moshe (22); Ramatz (13); She'el uMeshiv Kama (51); see Haflaah Ketubot 72 on Raavad brought in Rashba and Ran; Noda BiYehudah Even HaEzer 256 — "do not place wicked as witness" only when liable lashes; misleading others not disqualified for testimony — still suspected; see Ramatz (4).`,
    '2#_': `(2) One may not rely on him regarding them — meaning not to buy from him; but believed to permit others' property as Mechaber below seif 7 (see there s.k.): Taz s.k. 1; Shach s.k. 2; Peri Chadash s.k. 2; Shulchan Gavoah s.k. 3; Zivchei Tzedek s.k. 1.`,
    '3#א': `(3) If one dined with him he may not eat from matters he is suspected about — suspected on forbidden foods forbidden to eat with him even permitted foods lest he feed forbidden; Shabbat 11; 13; Ri'az in Shulchan Shel Rama first chapter Shabbat; Taz end s.k. 4 in Darkei Moshe from Hagahot Asheri; Kenesset HaGedolah; Beli; Maharalnach (121); Niddah 20; Rosh and Ran there.`,
    '3#ב': `(3) Peri Chadash end s.k. 6: Darkei Moshe from Hagahot Asheri not from core law — mere stringency and custom of pious; Lechem HaPanim s.k. 6; Shvilei David s.k. 1; question why not cite Shabbat Gemara — perhaps decree only for suspected on tithes common then; not for individual suspected; Bekor Shor on Avodah Zarah 39 — challenged Peri Chadash; Rambam 11:10 — if not careful forbidden to lodge; Mesgeret HaShulchan; Arukh HaShulchan; Eshkol HaShulchan on Rashi Avodah Zarah 38; Gra Bnei Brak Bet David 2; Maharsha responsa (7) — practical leniency; Zivchei Tzedek s.k. 5.`,
    '4#_': `(4) Rama: even from one not suspected but unrecognized — dispute Rambam/Raavad; Mechaber like Raavad — presume everyone even d'oraisa; Rashi Avodah Zarah 39; Meiri; Ritva; most Acharonim; Maharam Rotenburg (366); Beit She'arim (287); Rashba; Ritva Gittin 2; practice lenient on plain person; Rama in Darkei Moshe custom like Raavad; Shach s.k. 1; Ribash (121); Kenesset HaGedolah; Beli; Makom Shmuel; Knesset Yisrael; Kaf HaChayim Orach Chaim 20:2; Peri Toar leans stringent like Rambam — we follow Acharonim; see s.k. 6.`,
    '5#_': `(5) However for buying meat — not from every butcher who slaughters for himself and sells unless presumption of kosher — Mechaber siman 65; Shach s.k. 1 — many slaughter laws, easy to disqualify; Peri Chadash s.k. 1; Shulchan Gavoah s.k. 6; Peri Toar questions Shach — still stringent; Ramatz (12); Makom Shmuel no distinction meat vs other — stringent; Shach: slaughterer for others has presumption — only self-slaughterer needs presumption; Sefatei Daat 1:1; Arukh HaShulchan s.k. 8; Shvilei David s.k. 1; Maharam Schick (12); Chatam Sofer (19); Amudim Or (42); Nehar Efrayim (49).`,
    '6#_': `(6) Nevertheless l'chatchila though we are lenient buying from plain person — fear Rambam in this generation corruption; Taz s.k. 2; Beit Hillel siman 65 — registry requiring rabbinical certificate even for presumed kosher; lost certificate still rely if presumed; Beli; Lechem HaPanim s.k. 2; Kerem Shlomo; especially now; Arukh HaShulchan s.k. 9; Kenesset HaGedolah letter 8 from Rashdam 90 — if reason to fear without presumption, stringent; Beli; Mesgeret HaShulchan; Zivchei Tzedek s.k. 3; Chokhmat Adam 91:1 — buy meat wine milk bread only from recognized presumption lest "before the blind"; responsa Efrakasta DeAnya (70); Ramatz (12); Bekor Shor Avodah Zarah 39; Nahara Mitzrayim.`,
    '7#_': `(7) If one dined with him he may eat with him — not that l'chatchila forbidden to lodge to eat; even suspected for selling may dine seif 2; Mechaber "if one dined" teaches even b'dieved forbidden to eat from his suspected matters; Rama same b'dieved wording; Shach s.k. 4; Kenesset HaGedolah; Peri Chadash s.k. 4; Beli s.k. 3; Lechem HaPanim s.k. 4.`,
    '8#_': `(8) Suspected eater with God-fearing wife supervising kitchen — may lodge and eat if she prevents forbidden mixture; Avodah Zarah 39 — daughter of scholar or wife of scholar married to gentile presumed kosher until suspected; Rambam 10:3; Olat Yitzchak (184); Maharach Ruach Chaim s.k. 1 — saw scrupulous eat with suspected hosts because host not suspected to feed especially with careful wife; but without that cannot rely host not feeding — R' Yochanan Demai 2: on himself not believed how on others; Raah Bedek HaBayit believed more careful with others' — Rashba Meshoreret HaBayit rejected; Tashbetz 3:83; Tashbetz 1:63 prefers Rashi/Rashba forbid; Kehal Yehudah 1; Eshkol HaShulchan s.k. 3; Choshen Mishpat 34:47 — stringent; see s.k. 11.`,
    '9#_': `(9) [seif 2] Not suspected to eat but suspected to sell — may dine; plain suspected seller not suspected eater; suspected eater also suspected seller; Taz s.k. 3; Shach s.k. 5; Peri Chadash s.k. 5; Peri Toar s.k. 5; Lechem HaPanim s.k. 5; Mesgeret HaShulchan; Zivchei Tzedek s.k. 6; Maharsham 6:83; Daat Torah 2:25; Chakrei Lev 150.`,
    '10#_': `(10) May eat with him but forbidden to buy — even if gives to suspected to taste and suspected eats — forbidden to buy; suspected considers entire sale chain; buyer may tell taste; Peri Toar s.k. 2; Zivchei Tzedek s.k. 7; Yehuda Yaaleh Asad 51; Nehar Efrayim Even HaEzer 53 — kutim believed by law, only clarification; if found eating himself may buy; Peri Toar; Zivchei Tzedek.`,
    '11#_': `(11) Sending to his house permitted — Raah Bedek HaBayit permits even suspected eater; we do not hold so — Rashba Meshoreret HaBayit; Meiri; Tashbetz 3:83; Peri Chadash s.k. 6; Shulchan Gavoah s.k. 9; Mesgeret HaShulchan; Eshkol HaShulchan s.k. 3; perhaps if clarified he is not suspected to feed — Tashbetz 1:63; requires study; Raah forbade buying only permitted gift.`,
    '12#_': `(12) Rama: transgressing for appetite not called suspected — not suspected for that matter until transgresses not for appetite; here no concern he will now transgress for appetite; that time was for appetite; Shach s.k. 6; Peri Chadash s.k. 6 — known permitted/forbidden before him; not abandoning permitted; else forbidden; answers Taz s.k. 4; Nakahat HaKesef; Lechem HaPanim agrees Taz concedes; Shulchan Gavoah s.k. 9; Mesgeret HaShulchan; Makom Shmuel; Bet David Bistritz; Chokhmat Adam 91:4; Zivchei Tzedek s.k. 8; Arukh HaShulchan s.k. 13 wrong — follow Acharonim; Binyan Tziyon 10 supports Shach from Sanhedrin 9; Chakrei Lev appetite vs suspected; Beit Hillel siman 2 — nowadays suspected nevelah for appetite do not eat anything with prohibition concern; Kaf HaChayim siman 2 s.k. 24; see s.k. 46.`,
    '13#_': `(13) [seif 3] Permitted to give own food to suspected eater to prepare/cook — only in his vessels; forbidden in suspected's vessels; Peri Chadash s.k. 9; Kehal Yehudah; Zivchei Tzedek s.k. 9.`,
    '14#_': `(14) Not concerned he will substitute — Rama siman 118:1 deposit with suspected d'oraisa two seals d'rabbanan one — there suspected to substitute; abbreviated; or suspected on matters public not lenient siman 118:8 and here; Bach; Shach s.k. 7; see siman 118 s.k. 83.`,
    '15#_': `(15) Since not suspected of theft — suspected to sell still not suspected to steal/substitute by hand; sale instructs heter; Shach s.k. 8; Arukh HaShulchan s.k. 15; Zivchei Tzedek s.k. 10.`,
    '16#_': `(16) Mother-in-law suspected — Rambam 1:12 not suspected to substitute per Yerushalmi Demai ch.3 — halachah like Sages; Kesef Mishneh; Meiri Chullin 6 — women careful, presumption no spoiled dish; Mahari Kurkus; dissenters say like innkeeper — follow Rambam; Beit Hillel s.k. 1 wonders Mechaber against Rambam; Peri Toar explains Rambam — not feed forbidden to daughter; no clear ruling against Mechaber — lean lenient when doubt; Peri Chadash defends Mechaber — Rashba, Tur; Chatam Sofer 6:11; Mesgeret HaShulchan; practice Mechaber; Eshkol HaShulchan s.k. 16–17.`,
    '17#_': `(17) Ashamed before son-in-law wants daughter's benefit — also betrothed mother-in-law; Yerushalmi Demai ch.3; Eshkol HaShulchan s.k. 4; Zivchei Tzedek s.k. 11; relative or friend wanting his benefit — Rashba in Tur; Shulchan Gavoah s.k. 13.`,
    '18#_': `(18) Innkeeper — if known not ashamed of guest as modern innkeepers who care nothing for guest's food — permitted per Rashi Chullin 6; Rashba, Meiri; Rashbetz 1:144; practice Rashi; if pays wages perhaps not suspected — Shach s.k. 9; Peri Chadash; practice Rashi when not ashamed; if ashamed even with wages — forbidden; Zivchei Tzedek s.k. 12.`,
    '19#_': `(19) [seif 4] Suspected for one matter not for others — all above regarding suspected eater; suspected seller who violates "before the blind" even less severe prohibition or different type — suspected on severe "before the blind" — Netah Shorek 44; Efrakasta DeAnya 70; I disagree — if other prohibition more severe in his eyes not suspected misleading others; poskim seif 7; Arukh HaShulchan s.k. 22; Chakrei Lev 150, 162 stringent; Zivchei Tzedek s.k. 94; lean stringent; see s.k. 118.`,
    '20#_': `(20) [seif 4] Everything needed for that matter also suspected — suspected selling milk entices with nuts penalized not to sell even nuts; all needed for that sale suspected; suspected seller on severe forbidden in eyes also on light in same food category; if different categories not; suspected for two matters who repented then suspected on one — suspected on both even if lighter; Chakrei Lev 150.`,
  },
  'mateh-yehonatan': {
    '1#_': `(siman 119 in Shulchan Aruch seif 1) One suspected of eating, etc. As explained in Shach s.k. 1 — but it is difficult, for in the new simanim in siman 1 the Mechaber ruled like Rambam at length; one must say there the matter does not depend on presumption of kosher status, for certainly the Mechaber holds one does not remove a person from presumption of kosher status; there the reason is we say he is not expert in a matter he thinks he is expert in all his laws and is not — therefore we do not remove him from presumption of kosher status; investigate.`,
    '2#_': `(there seif 7) And regarding others' property, etc. They challenge from what we learned in chapter 4 of Demai: one who buys produce from one not trusted regarding tithes and forgot to tithe and asked on Shabbat — may eat on his word; after Shabbat dark he may not eat until he tithes, etc. — if so we learn even regarding others he is not believed at all. One must say there it deals when he said generically and did not say they are tithed; he said he saw he tithed the produce — he saw they are tithed for that produce; if so we are concerned he speaks in the language of Demai thinking they are tithed but in truth they are not — unlike if the suspected one said he saw they were tithed, one may say in truth even though he is suspected, regarding others we do not suspect him to lie intentionally. The Mechaber wrote he is believed regarding others — one may say when he said he saw the act, and this is believed by all; see Lechem Mishneh chapter 1 of Forbidden Foods chapter 3 law 21 see there.`,
    '3#_': `(there in the gloss seif 7) One who is suspected, etc.; however regarding that matter he is not believed. As explained in Shach s.k. 9 who wrote the Rav's words need explanation, etc. see there; and I say to explain: certainly if one is suspected of eating one prohibition he is not suspected for every kind of prohibition only for that matter alone — if suspected of eating the "pants" of Rhine children where for them it is a karet prohibition we do not say he is suspected for all milk in the world, only we say he is believed for all milk except that matter itself, namely stomach-milk, regarding which he is not believed at all; investigate.`,
    '4#_': `(there seif 19 in the gloss) And all this does not apply to one suspected of prohibition, etc. See Shach s.k. 37 end of the seif; still difficult, for in siman 86 the Rav wrote even a gentile selling eggs is believed when he says this is from such-and-such bird and pure though it is d'oraisa; one must say there in Avodah Zarah 63b the baraita permits buying eggs from gentiles everywhere without concern for nevelah or tereifah; Ran asks since most birds are pure why challenge; answer: minority of nevelah and tereifah combine with minority of impure bird for half-and-half — Ran; Tosafot; and it is known wherever there is fruit the minority is forbidden only d'rabbanan; therefore he is believed saying this bird and pure — requires study. [His words require study, for Ran wrote half-and-half or majority — if so it is d'oraisa doubt; requires study.]`,
  },
  'mechaber': {
    '1#main': `One who is suspected regarding a forbidden matter may not be relied upon regarding edible matters. It contains 20 seifim: One who is suspected of eating forbidden things — whether he is suspected of a d'oraisa prohibition or a d'rabbanan prohibition — may not be relied upon regarding them; and if one dines with him he may not eat from his food regarding matters he is suspected about: {Rama: And some say even from one who is not suspected but whom they do not recognize as having a presumption of kosher status — it is forbidden to buy wine or other things where there is concern of prohibition from him; however if one dines with him one may eat with him (Beit Yosef in the name of Rambam chapter 1 of Forbidden Foods and chapter 3 law 21):}`,
    '2#main': `If he is not suspected of eating forbidden things but is suspected of selling them — one may dine with him and eat with him; and likewise if he sends to his house it is permitted, for the presumption is that from what he himself eats he sends to him. {Rama: (One who transgresses a sin for appetite is not called suspected) (Beit Yosef in the name of Rashba):}`,
    '3#main': `It is permitted to give from one's own food to one suspected of eating forbidden things so that he may prepare or cook for him, and we are not concerned lest he substitute, since he is not suspected of theft; but if one gives to someone who is concerned about his preparation and he is suspected of eating forbidden things — it is forbidden, lest what he gives become spoiled and he substitute his own. How so? If one gave to his mother-in-law who is suspected regarding the prohibition — it is forbidden, for she is ashamed before her son-in-law and wants her daughter's benefit and therefore substitutes bad for good; and likewise one who gives to an innkeeper who is suspected — sometimes she is ashamed before the guest and substitutes bad for good. {Rama: (Nevertheless it is permitted to deposit with him and that he return the items as deposited):}`,
    '4#main': `One suspected regarding one matter is not suspected regarding other matters; but everything needed for that matter he is also suspected regarding — such as one who was suspected of selling milk in a place of fat and would entice the youths to come buy from him with nuts that he gave them — they penalize him that he may not sell even nuts.`,
    '5#main': `One suspected regarding a severe prohibition is suspected regarding a lighter one than it in penalty, unless the severe one is more stringent in people's eyes such that they are more careful about it than the severe one.`,
    '6#main': `One suspected regarding two matters who repented and left suspicion of both of them, and afterward was suspected regarding one of them — even if it is the lighter of the two — we are concerned lest he returned to his way regarding both and he is suspected regarding both.`,
    '7#main': `One who is notorious for one of the transgressions in the Torah except idolatry and public desecration of Shabbat, or who does not believe in the words of our Sages of blessed memory — is believed regarding other prohibitions; and regarding others' property he is believed even regarding that matter to say it is permitted: {Rama: One who is suspected regarding a matter that does not appear to people as a transgression is not called suspected (Rashba siman 64); however regarding that matter he is not believed. One who practices prohibition in some matter because he holds the law is so, or because of a stringency he adopted — may eat with others who practice leniency regarding it, for they will certainly not feed him what he practices as forbidden (Hagahot Mordechai first chapter of Yevamot and Binyamin Zeev 312 and R' Yerucham end Netiv 3 in the name of Rama):}`,
    '8#main': `One suspected regarding the matter is not believed regarding it even under oath.`,
    '9#main': `A convert from gentiles who returned and apostatized out of fear, and likewise an Israelite who sinned out of fear lest they kill him — is a full Israelite and his slaughter is valid and he does not forbid wine by contact.`,
    '10#main': `An informer — his slaughter is valid and he is believed regarding prohibitions {Rama: (and see above siman 2 that some disqualify an informer's slaughter):}`,
    '11#main': `An apostate in one city who believes in idolatry before gentiles and in another city enters a Jewish home and says he is a Jew — does not make libation wine.`,
    '12#main': `The forced ones who remained in their lands — if they conduct themselves with kosher practice among themselves and cannot escape to a place where they can serve God — we rely on their slaughter and do not forbid wine by their contact.`,
    '13#main': `One who sells his fellow something forbidden to eat — if before he ate it became known, he returns what he bought from him and he returns the money; and if after he ate it became known — what he ate he ate and he returns the money; and if the buyer sold it to a gentile or threw it to dogs he pays him the value of tereifah; and if it was something not forbidden to eat but of rabbinic origin, or if the produce still exists — he returns the produce and takes its value; and if he ate them — what he ate he ate and the seller returns nothing; and all benefit-prohibitions even of rabbinic origin — he returns the money and there is no law of sale at all.`,
    '14#main': `One who sold meat and afterward it became known the animal was not checked properly — its law is like one who sold things forbidden by rabbinic enactment: {Rama: And if he sold him something from an animal whose presumption of kosher status was impaired such that a defect arose in it and they cannot check whether it is kosher and must forbid it out of doubt — even so it is as if it were certainly tereifah and he must return the money (responsum of Ribash):}`,
    '15#main': `One who sells forbidden things — they remove him and excommunicate him and he has no remedy until he goes to a place where he is not recognized and returns a significant lost object or slaughters for himself and produces tereifah for himself in a significant matter such that he certainly repented without subterfuge since he is not sparing of his money.`,
    '16#main': `A butcher whose actions prove he wished to mislead the public to feed them tereifot — such as one who cut away adhesions — they remove him; nevertheless he is exempt from paying the animal's value to the owners.`,
    '17#main': `A butcher from whom tereifah issued — he has no excuse to say he was inadvertent.`,
    '18#main': `A butcher from whom tereifot issued because he is not expert — he has a remedy that he learn and become wise: {Rama: And that which he must go to a place where he is not recognized and repent — that is when he acted intentionally or has a presumption of such; but if we can say it happened in error, it suffices with accepting fellowship rules and repenting according to what the judge sees fit (responsum of Rosh and responsum of Ribash) and see above end of siman 64:}`,
    '19#main': `One who says to one suspected of eating gentile cheese: buy me kosher cheese from the expert — and he went and brought it and says: I bought kosher from the expert — he is not believed; from so-and-so the expert I bought — he is believed. If he brought him a gift in the name of one of the experts — he is believed that he is not suspected to substitute. In what case? When he is not suspected of theft; but if he is suspected of theft — all the more so he is suspected of substitution. {Rama: (And all this does not apply to one suspected of a d'oraisa prohibition) (Beit Yosef):}`,
    '20#main': `One suspected of eating things the public are not accustomed to be lenient regarding — he is also suspected to substitute; and some say one does not deposit with him a d'oraisa forbidden item except with two seals {Rama: (and see above siman 118 seif 8):}`,
  },
  'nekudot-hakesef': {
    '1#_': `(siman 119 in Taz s.k. 4) And his words are astounding in this gloss. See in Shach s.k. 6 I settled his words on the matter that they are correct for practical halachah.`,
    '2#_': `(there in Shach s.k. 7) And in Bach he explained, etc., and milk and morayis and bread and cheese and the like — that requiring one seal does not deal with except a gentile, but one deposits with a suspected person even without a seal, etc.; so one must say and so in Bach there see there.`,
    '3#_': `(there in Taz s.k. 7) Nevertheless there is a difficulty on Beit Yosef who wrote it is known the halachah is like R' Shimon, etc.; Bach already preceded this.`,
    '4#_': `(there s.k. 8) Likewise in the Perishah. See Shach s.k. 13 he strained for nothing.`,
    '5#_': `(there s.k. 9) Also in this it is wondrous, etc.; one must say as I wrote in Shach s.k. 19.`,
    '6#_': `(there s.k. 12) It appears from this that even if the prohibition is safek tereifah, etc. — meaning even safek tereifah which is forbidden only in eating; likewise explicit in his words in notes 13–14; not clear a contradiction that the seller will say it is not kosher and the buyer must bring proof; Rashi deals with a firstborn forbidden in benefit — likewise d'rabbanan forbidden in benefit requires returning money, for benefit-prohibition has no law of sale at all; d'rabbanan prohibition and safek prohibition share one law; further it appears there regarding firstborn it is like not checking the lung which is a rabbinic enactment — likewise showing a firstborn is their enactment as below siman 309; on the contrary showing firstborn is stricter for we find in mishnah and Gemara one must show a sage; if so it is like something forbidden in benefit d'rabbanan; what Rashi wrote safek prohibition means therefore they penalized him because of safek prohibition they touched it — meaning the Sages enacted thus out of doubt; likewise lung checking they enacted out of doubt; know that Rashi's intent is as I explained and this is clear. I again found as my words in Semak Choshen Mishpat siman 234: only for certain tereifah; but for safek tereifah he need not return money if eaten; and a slaughterer who sold a cow and it became known tereifah — he returns even if already eaten — meaning certainly tereifah; and firstborn differs for an animal is presumed without defect; also firstborn not checked — even if afterward known it had a defect — still forbidden in benefit d'rabbanan as mishnah chapter Ad Kamah and Tur Yoreh Deah siman 310; since it is benefit-prohibition the seller does not deduct from payment and the reason is sensible: even if intact the seller would have to discard and not benefit — therefore no law of sale; as above; and clear from his words even if the seller knew before sale it is safek tereifah he need not return if eaten; if he did not know, even certain tereifah they do not penalize as Shach s.k. 25.`,
    '7#_': `(there in Taz s.k. 15) He explained even though Shulchan Aruch wrote, etc. This explanation is not so as Shach s.k. 29 and I proved nearby it is not so.`,
    '8#_': `(there end s.k. 18) He extended to challenge — meaning even d'oraisa prohibitions the law is so; according to Shach s.k. 37 one must say see there.`,
  },
  'pitchei-teshuva': {
    '1#_': `They will not feed him. See responsa Chaim Shaal part 2 who wrote in the name of Derech HaMelech: one who has chametz that passed Pesach and others come to eat from it — since for them no penalty applies he need not tell them not to eat; one sage disagrees; the author agreed one must inform them; see there.`,
    '2#_': `And in another city. Pri Toar wrote only when he has no benefit from saying he is Jewish, etc.; see Noda BiYehudah second part Even HaEzer beginning siman 80 who wrote his words are not compelling and even per his view this is only to rely leniently that his wine contact is permitted, but to be stringent, etc.; see there.`,
    '3#_': `The money. Baer Heitev; see responsa Bashamayim Rosh siman 97 and Hagahot Kasav there; see Mishnat Chachamim Laws of Idolatry daf 103 in Tzafnat Paaneach letter 168: if he fed his fellow's forbidden item his law is like one who steals and feeds — sometimes collects from the feeder and sometimes from the eater; if the victim collects from the eater it seems the eater cannot return to the feeder saying he fed forbidden and it is not considered eating therefore the feeder pays; the law of feeding others is not like a seller — requires study, end of his words.`,
    '4#_': `His actions prove. See responsa Beit Efrayim Yoreh Deah siman 1 regarding a slaughterer about whom rumor spread — clearly our speech is not removing the slaughterer from his presumption even for an incessant rumor, for any rumor not established in court is nothing; all the more here the rumor is a woman's gossip, etc.; also wrote it appears even a scrupulous person need not be concerned though we say this language is bad, etc. — only where there is no excuse to attribute the rumor as false and perhaps true; but where there is an excuse that out of spite unnamed people spread slander — no concern for that rumor at all; see there.`,
    '5#_': `I was inadvertent. Shach; see responsa Pri Tevuah 12 and 14; see what is written above siman 1 s.k. 4 in the name of Ateret Zahav in Peri Megadim see there.`,
    '6#_': `And that which he must, etc. See responsa Beit Efrayim Yoreh Deah part siman 5 what he wrote on this.`,
  },
  'siftei-kohen': {
    '1#א': `["One suspected of eating, etc. It appears that not relying on him is only when known he is suspected; but on a plain Israelite we rely even on d'oraisa prohibition, as Tur wrote. In what case? A suspected person; but a plain person has presumption of kosher to eat with him — likewise Raavad end chapter 1 of Maaser; likewise Rashi wrote Syrian merchants are suspected; likewise R' Levi ibn Chaviv responsum 121; I also found proof from Yerushalmi chapter 8 of Shevi'it law 2: R' Yosi said they only spoke when one does not know if he is suspected; if clearly suspected — forbidden, etc. Not like Rambam (and he is the \\"some say\\" the Rav brought) who requires recognizing presumption of kosher status; likewise Darkei Moshe — custom unlike Rambam, presume everyone kosher. Difficult: in new simanim siman 1 Mechaber ruled like Rambam requiring trustworthy slaughterer; Beit Yosef there unlike Mordechai that plain Israelite is presumed kosher; and siman 65 Mechaber/Tur/Rambam — do not buy meat from butcher who slaughters for himself and sells without presumption; difficult also on Tur citing Rambam in siman 65; Taz distinguishes slaughtering butcher — common, many slaughter laws, easy disqualifications — if no presumption forbidden."]`,
    '1#ב': `["One may not rely on him regarding them — meaning to buy from him; but they rely when he says regarding others it is permitted, as Mechaber seif 7; see there and s.k. 5."]`,
    '1#ג': `["Forbidden to buy wine or other matters of prohibition concern — some say other literal Torah prohibitions; but even wine — stam yeinam is only d'rabbanan decreed lest definite libation wine; some say not only wine but anything with prohibition concern even entirely d'rabbanan like cheese and gentile bread where custom forbids; from Rav's conclusion likewise milk — not bought unless presumption of kosher; unlike Rambam there who permits milk from anyone — Maggid Mishneh learned from version lacking milk among Syrian restrictions; in our texts Chullin Avodah Zarah 39b includes milk with fish piece — Tosafot and Beit Yosef; Rav's view: milk like other matters."]`,
    '1#ד': `["However if one dined with him he may eat with him — not worse than suspected for selling forbidden (Taz next seif); Mechaber \\"if one dined\\" does not mean l'chatchila forbidden to lodge to eat — even suspected for selling may dine (Taz); Mechaber uses \\"if one dined\\" to teach even b'dieved after lodging forbidden to eat from suspected matters."]`,
    '2#א': `["If he is not suspected to eat forbidden but suspected to sell — Rashi and Ran: only suspected to sell because \\"before the blind\\" — sells from gentile but he himself does not eat forbidden; if one dined permitted; if sends to house permitted — presumption he sends what he eats."]`,
    '2#ב': `["Transgressing for appetite not called suspected — not suspected for that matter until transgresses not for appetite; if forbidden and permitted before him he would eat forbidden; some say habitual nevelah eater for appetite — forbidden from his slaughter until knife checked siman 2 — knives often nicked, fear transgression for appetite; here no concern he will now transgress for appetite — that time was for appetite; now why transgress — not called suspected."]`,
    '3#א': `["Not concerned he will substitute — Tur/Rav siman 118:1 deposit with suspected Israelite d'oraisa two seals d'rabbanan one — there suspected to substitute; abbreviated not explaining who is suspected; Bach: there suspected on matters public not lenient (as Mechaber siman 118:8 and here); milk, morayis, bread, cheese deposited with suspected even without seal."]`,
    '3#ב': `["Since not suspected of theft — also suspected to sell because suspected to eat (above seif 1); only for selling he instructs heter; not suspected to steal or substitute by hand."]`,
    '3#ג': `["Innkeeper sometimes ashamed — Taz: Chullin 6 explains innkeeper instructs heter for herself good, guest hot/cold comfortably; Rashba: where known not ashamed of guest as modern innkeepers — permitted; Tosafot opposite — takes good, gives guest bad because she toiled; where he pays wages perhaps permitted; Tosafot Shevuot 48 and Mordechai — agent with wages cannot impose oath for then no heter instruction; even Rambam chapter 9 Sheluchin — even with wages can impose oath lest wages improper — perhaps concedes innkeeper wages."]`,
    '4#א': `["But everything needed for that matter also suspected — even without prohibition concern suspected regarding sale lest through it sell prohibition; as we penalize not to sell even nuts."]`,
    '4#ב': `["Such as suspected selling milk in fat place enticing youths with nuts — Bach: butcher suspected on milk suspected on all prohibitions even more severe than milk — matter public not lenient; Shach challenged — learned from Rashba only suspected on matter public not lenient to substitute that matter, not another; Rashba Meshoreret HaBayit explicit; Chullin 5a — suspected milk eater not suspected other matters; Tosafot s.v. from common people; see siman 4."]`,
    '5#א': `["Suspected on severe prohibition suspected on lighter in penalty unless severe more stringent in people's eyes — Tur: suspected on severe also on lighter even if lighter more severe in penalty if not more in people's eyes; Mechaber perhaps: if severe in penalty then also on lighter if equal in people's eyes; if equal in penalty but severe in people's eyes — suspected on lighter in people's eyes; if severe only in people's eyes not on lighter if lighter more severe in penalty — perhaps honors Heaven more; judge leniently both ways; all only same prohibition type both food etc.; else no; Ramban responsum 170; Rashba 64; Beit Yosef; Mechaber siman 2 — habitual circumcision sinner not slaughter sinner though circumcision stricter; Perishah: only with benefit from lighter; Shach: Perishah not compelling."]`,
    '5#ב': `["Suspected on lighter — Perishah: only with benefit; siman 2 only idolatry and public Shabbat desecration make habitual sinner for all; other sins not — Perishah strained; siman 2: idolatry/Shabbat worst; other sins rule as here if lighter with benefit — plain."]`,
    '6#_': `Even if lighter of the two — even two prohibition types if suspected again on one even lighter — suspected on more severe because initially suspected on both; Mechaber wrote two matters not two prohibitions contrasting previous seif.`,
    '7#א': `["Except idolatry and public Shabbat — explained siman 2 habitual for entire Torah; know habitual libation wine sinner also for entire Torah — Chullin chapter 1 and all poskim — included in idolatry habitual."]`,
    '7#ב': `["Or does not believe in Sages — like idolater; Rashba responsum Beit Yosef; explained siman 2 habitual for rebellion like idolater; see siman 218:2."]`,
    '7#ג': `["Believed in other prohibitions — even own, even sell, even eat with him."]`,
    '7#ד': `["Believed on others even that matter to say permitted — only prohibition; monetary: suspected on one matter disqualified testify on another even others' — Choshen Mishpat 34; difficulties Rama vs Rav on testimony; halachah like Rashbag against R' Meir Yoma end Bechorot 39b; Rashba 64; Ribash 4; Rambam; Semag; Ran 25; Ribash 4,12; Rosh; Tur; all poskim siman 154; Choshen Mishpat 34 on woman testimony — interest; siman 127 gloss; suspected vs habitual sinner siman 2,127 — habitual not believed others' that matter."]`,
    '7#ה': `["Suspected in matter not appearing to people as transgression — not called suspected to be believed even under oath; not believed that matter without oath — Rashba responsum 124; printed responsa Rav siman 124 supports: not called suspected without oath on that matter but not believed without oath; Rama: others will not feed what he forbids — only when others know he practices prohibition; Rama siman 112:15 may eat with non-careful because hatred — bread main meal lenient; Maharar Levi ibn Chaviv 121 summarized laws: practices prohibition thinking law so, eats in permitter's house — if recognizable to forbearer permitter may feed; if not recognizable forbidden; forbearer cannot tell permitter cook in non-ben-yomo vessels; permitter may cook l'chatchila for him and others b'dieved; forbearer by ancestral custom cannot be fed even if recognizable — like handing wine to nazir; forbearer may cook l'chatchila in permitter's non-ben-yomo vessels; ask vessel owner if present; suspected vessel owner need not ask if he volunteers; forbearer cannot eat permitter's cooking if suspected and dish may contain that fat; plain ignoramus forbearer may lodge if knows host forbids that food; custom places — Rhine stomach-milk custom vs lenient places — Polish visitor forbidden unless food custom not feeder-only custom; forbidden by custom only without seeing leniency — if sees leniency coming from prohibition place to leniency place may eat even intending return; R' Yehuda David end seif."]`,
    '7#ו': `["Suspected on matter not believed even under oath — already suspected to transgress oath being under oath from Sinai; even Sinai oath for past since under oath and sinned worse; no future/past oath distinction except money not prohibition — see Choshen Mishpat 92:2, 87:15; all the more not believed on lighter matter under oath; Maharshal Chullin 6: suspected stam yeinam nowadays may be believed on yayin nesech with oath specifying not under Sinai oath because lenient on stam yeinam d'rabbanan — ruled practice; perhaps only stam yeinam because lenient; do not rule other d'rabbanan; cheese not believed even under oath — d'rabbanan also under Sinai \\"do not turn aside\\"; Rashba 64; Rashba 109 perhaps stam yeinam more lenient in his day than cheese."]`,
    '8#א': `["Suspected on matter not believed even under oath — reason above."]`,
    '8#ב': `["Not believed on lighter under oath — Maharshal stam yeinam yayin nesech with specified oath as above."]`,
    '10#_': `Informer — slaughter valid, believed on prohibitions; see siman 2 s.k. 26.`,
    '11#_': `Apostate in one city — reason explained: gentile statement plausible false; saying he believes for evil inclination; saying Jewish heartfelt true faith; plain not believed prohibitions except does not make yayin nesech being truly Jewish; worships idols for desire — not believed.`,
    '13#א': `["What he ate he ate — penalty Rashi Bechorot end Kol Pesulei HaMukdashin; only when known knew forbidden and sold intentionally penalize; else perhaps inadvertent; seif 17 butcher no excuse — prohibition not money; Taz: even safek tereifah ate and returns money, eating not benefit."]`,
    '13#ב': `["He shall pay — since did not cause prohibition. Rashi."]`,
    '13#ג': `["If ate — d'oraisa penalized; d'rabbanan eating considered benefit; money established on Torah law — Sages did not decree prohibition to extract money; Rambam chapter 16 Sales; Yerushalmi Shevi'it — d'oraisa forbidden commerce d'rabbanan permitted; same here sale; see Choshen Mishpat 234."]`,
    '14#א': `["Animal not checked properly — siman 39:2 lung lost once coercion/inadvertence OK; intentional unchecked eater forbidden though rabbinic checking — like nullifying prohibition l'chatchila; Mechaber 99:6; Taz: butcher Choshen Mishpat 306 exempt doubt because obligate his act — only certainly; buyer-seller even doubt mistaken transaction; Achai needle after cutting — not per above penalty reason; perhaps eaten before known tereifah; or needle swallowed before purchase; Choshen Mishpat 232:11 within three days buyer proves; if seller's permission after slaughter clear mistaken transaction; if ate butcher exempt no penalty; mistaken transaction while meat remains return defect; seller knew — returns even if ate unlike Levush."]`,
    '14#ב': `["Sold from animal with defect — meaning not checked properly certainly forbidden by their words must return while meat exists; even sold with defect requiring safek forbid — seller cannot say perhaps not tereifah bring proof — as certainly tereifah by their words return while exists; any safek tereifot voids transaction if meat exists; if ate seller returns nothing; Ribash 299; Beit Yosef; Choshen Mishpat 232:12; Bach misunderstood Mechaber; Taz: defect requires check d'oraisa cannot check — even if ate return d'oraisa safek; seller knew penalty; if not know mistaken transaction only if not eaten; Shach disagrees — if ate seller says return sale and money; Semak 234:1; Rama explanation as above; Ribash/Ramban/Chullin Rashba agree."]`,
    '15#א': `["Sells forbidden — remove even first time; Mechaber seif 17; Choshen Mishpat 306:9 some say butcher/teacher need presumption — Ribash only monetary loss or misled; suspected feeding prohibition remove immediately; Rashba butcher tereifah no inadvertent excuse; vessels siman 1; Maharshal once transgression stumbling block; forbid vessels retroactively doubt even first time."]`,
    '15#ב': `["Go where unrecognized — Choshen Mishpat 34."]`,
    '16#_': `Exempt from paying — only safek tereifot; Ribash; Choshen Mishpat 306:5.`,
    '17#_': `No inadvertent excuse — Mahari Weil God-fearing person — certainly erred, not removed.`,
    '19#א': `["Buy kosher cheese from expert — meaning even said buy cheese and said bought from expert not believed unless said from so-and-so expert — fears lie; even sender did not instruct specific expert — believed if says from expert so-and-so."]`,
    '19#ב': `["From so-and-so expert believed — fears ask so-and-so; even sender did not instruct; even said buy cheese and from expert so-and-so believed."]`,
    '19#ג': `["Gift in expert name — Tur Rashba not suspected substitute; person does not give own gift in another's name; must mention expert name; plain expert sent gift believed; not fear wrong expert — would intend mislead; Rashba Tehillim 118; siman 118 different — perhaps no expert available; here different — person does not give own in another's name; if said not from expert loses money fears lie; there slaughter experts common; here purchase worse; siman 2 appetite transgressor not before blind; suspected thinks permitted — not believed d'oraisa; Perishah self-ate slaughterer refuted siman 2."]`,
    '19#ד': `["All this not regarding suspected d'oraisa prohibition — Beit Yosef first law; Rama same gift law d'oraisa; Perishah; Taz siman 2 slaughter d'oraisa believed; siman 86 eggs gentile; siman 118 thigh d'oraisa reasoning; Shach answers; Taz thigh challenge; practical study d'oraisa reasoning."]`,
    '20#_': `Suspected eating matters public not lenient — also substitute; some say deposit d'oraisa two seals — Raah Bedek HaBayit challenged; Rashba Meshoreret HaBayit: suspected on matter public not lenient not habitual for that matter — public lenient not habitual; suspected public matter not deposited without two seals; not like Raah; practical suspected one matter not other; Taz seif 2 Bach misunderstood Rashba; Tur wondered Stam; plain; see siman 151:4.`,
  },
  'turei-zahav': {
    '1#א': `One may not rely on him. Explanation: to buy from him.`,
    '1#ב': `And some say even from one who is not suspected, etc. Darkei Moshe wrote: however the custom is not so, but we presume every person in a presumption of kosher status; and since the Rav did not write here it appears we should not practice so; and it is correct, especially as we see corruption of generations and I do not presume the generation kosher.`,
    '2#א': `One dines with him. For even though he is suspected — "before the blind do not place a stumbling block" — nevertheless he himself is not suspected to eat it.`,
    '2#ב': `Transgressing a sin for appetite. The honor of the Rav is in its place and his words are astounding in this gloss, for in Rashba that Beit Yosef brought it is written that one who transgresses one of the transgressions is believed in other prohibitions, and all the more if he does so for appetite, etc.; if so why did the Rav write this gloss — if he is presumed to transgress other prohibitions, even not for appetite he is believed for this prohibition; and if presumed regarding this prohibition, even for appetite certainly he is not believed. What Beit Yosef wrote below in this siman in the name of Rashba that a habitual sinner for appetite is believed even for that matter, like Rava in the first chapter of Chullin, end of his words — meaning there is leniency when permitted and forbidden are before him he does not abandon the permitted and eat forbidden; but otherwise forbidden, as Tur siman 2 wrote that without knife checking forbidden to eat from his slaughter; here too how may they eat with him lest there was no permitted before him and he ate forbidden — how may others eat with him; if so this is not called suspected as the Rav said, and certainly this gloss has no explanation. What is harder: we never find that suspected is worse than transgressing for appetite — if you say there he is suspected meaning he instructs so intentionally even when permitted is before him he abandons it and eats forbidden, then he is like a full gentile, as Rama wrote in siman 2 regarding one who eats nevelah not for appetite; how can Rashbag in chapter Ad Kamah rule that one suspected regarding something may judge and testify. Also proof from what Terumat HaDeshen wrote: if you suspect him for this you suspect him as a full gentile intending to mislead, end of his words; and if you say suspected means he is not concerned for prohibition at all, in truth he is like a full gentile as we said; plainly he is not worse than transgressing for appetite — if so what does it mean he is not called suspected. Darkei Moshe in Hagahot Asheri: one suspected of a prohibition forbidden to eat with him — even permitted things, lest he feed forbidden too, end of his words.`,
    '3#_': `Behold he gave to his mother-in-law suspected regarding the prohibition — forbidden, for she is ashamed before her son-in-law, etc.; so it must be.`,
    '4#א': `Suspected also regarding it. Explanation: penalty — suspected that he will not sell at all even if he did not want to sell only for himself.`,
    '4#ב': `To come buy, etc. In chapter Ad Kamah (Bechorot 30) the Gemara: that butcher was suspected to sell fat, etc.; Rava penalized him even regarding nuts, etc.; difficult for Tur why he wrote the prohibition was with nuts that enticed youths — the Gemara's style differs; we need two elements: sale done through sin, and penalty for future suspicion; Tur notes what was needed for sinful sale; Gemara notes what connects to later suspicion via nuts; we penalize for teaching theft perhaps; Tur did not need to say he enticed youths — proved from "children run after nuts"; end of my explanation reconciling Tur and Gemara. Difficulty on Beit Yosef who wrote halachah is like R' Shimon — in Eruvin we conclude like R' Yehudah against R' Shimon as Rosh wrote; nevertheless here Beit Yosef well aimed halachah like R' Shimon for another reason since Rava holds like him as I said.`,
    '5#_': `Suspected regarding the light. Explanation: when he has benefit from it he is not believed to buy from him; but without his benefit we say a habitual sinner for one matter is not a habitual sinner for other transgressions except Shabbat and idolatry as seif 12; likewise Perishah; and Beit Yosef end of this siman distinguishes one food type vs two types.`,
    '7#_': `One who is suspected regarding a matter, etc. Also wondrous is Rama's view to me — Rashba in responsum Beit Yosef brought only for testimony disqualification, as he brings later for cemetery on eve of festival; but for reliability to eat with him or buy from him — permitted in other matters even intentional transgression knowing it is forbidden; if regarding that matter he does not appear as transgression to people, plainly others should not rely since he thinks it permitted — as Rama concludes here; nevertheless what is the practical difference — he is not called suspected.`,
    '8#_': `Even under oath. For he is already suspected to transgress the oath being under oath from Sinai; and even though Sinai oath is for past, since under oath and also sinned he is worse; and no distinction future vs past oath except monetary matters, not prohibition.`,
    '11#_': `He does not make libation wine. The reason: a gentile's statement is plausible as false; when he says he believes it is for evil inclination's pleasure and our faith is good and true; and when he tells us he is a Jew he says wholeheartedly because our faith is faithful and true and the matter is plain that for all prohibitions he is not believed; only he does not make yayin nesech being truly a Jew; but for reliability in prohibitions since he worships idols for desire plainly not believed.`,
    '13#א': `What he ate he ate, etc. So in mishnah Bechorot end of chapter Kol Pesulei HaMukdashin; Rashi: returns money because of penalty of safek prohibition; appears only when known he knew they were forbidden and sold intentionally we penalize; if not, perhaps inadvertent; and though seif 17 butcher has no excuse — that is prohibition not money; Taz appears even safek tereifah — what he ate he ate and returns money, eating not considered benefit.`,
    '13#ב': `Rabbinic matters. If produce still exists — so it must be; this is Rambam's view that in d'rabbanan prohibition once he ate and benefited he does not return.`,
    '14#א': `Its law like selling rabbinic prohibitions. Reason: d'oraisa no lung check needed without defect before us — we rely on majority kosher animals; Sages required checking; what he ate seller deducts; nevertheless what not eaten he returns for mistaken transaction; though regarding butcher Choshen Mishpat exempt in doubt because you come for his act — only obligate certainly; here between buyer and seller even doubt is mistaken transaction; Achai incident needle in lung after cutting — compared to butcher case — not per my reason; nevertheless well judged perhaps eaten before known tereifah; or needle swallowed before purchase; if within seller's permission after slaughter clearly mistaken transaction; if ate selling butcher exempt — no penalty since he did not know; unlike mistaken transaction while meat remains must return for defect; if seller knew and sold — buyer returns even if ate, unlike Levush.`,
    '14#ב': `And if he sold from an animal, etc. Even though Shulchan Aruch said if tereifah from improper lung check and buyer ate seller deducts — only where d'oraisa no check needed without defect; if defect exists automatically requires check d'oraisa and cannot check — even if buyer ate seller returns for selling doubt d'oraisa prohibition; only if seller knew doubt then penalty; if not, return only if not eaten from mistaken transaction.`,
    '15#_': `To a place where he is not recognized, etc. Maharshal: nowadays generation is unrestrained and if sages see he cannot exile because dependents hang on him they give repentance locally with restriction; one transgression makes him a stumbling block — warn him; nevertheless forbid vessels retroactively in doubt even first time; Shach: even first sale of forbidden removes him; Mechaber seif 17; Choshen Mishpat some say butcher and teacher need presumption before removal — Ribash: there only monetary loss or misled/inadvertent; suspected feeding prohibition — remove immediately; Rashba: butcher tereifah no inadvertent excuse; vessels siman 1; Beit Yosef Choshen Mishpat siman 34 Rambam: inspector for self needs exile after repentance; for others no monetary benefit — repentance suffices.`,
    '18#_': `By accepting fellowship rules. Explained in Bechorot chapter Ad Kamah that he accepts ascetic practices the majority do not practice.`,
    '19#_': `And all this does not apply to suspected, etc. Written per Beit Yosef — perhaps they were lenient only in their prohibitions; after Meshoreret HaBayit was revealed we see Rashba siman 2 regarding habitual sinner for appetite who said expert slaughtered — believed in d'oraisa slaughter; likewise Meshoreret HaBayit explicitly even d'oraisa we rely when he says explicitly so-and-so expert, for he fears; proof from Demai; cheese learns from Demai; all learn Demai; suspected not worse than habitual sinner for appetite; Rashba only requires explicit expert for cheese and slaughter; second law gift — person does not give from own in another's name — even d'oraisa as siman 118 thigh; Beit Yosef thought Rashba only on gentile cheese d'rabbanan — nothing, Rashba teaches even d'rabbanan need explicit expert; second law not only fear — even strong-handed person; gift-giver mentions expert's name; Perishah wrongly split d'oraisa vs d'rabbanan — refuted by Meshoreret HaBayit; resolves difficulties siman 2 and Demai and thigh; Beit Yosef brought Rambam believed on others' tithes — challenged from Demai mishnah; answer collusion concern; after Shabbat must tithe; Rashba believed on others' testimony; difficult Rambam believed in all cases — Raavad, Kesef Mishneh, Yerushalmi guest leniency; Tosafot guest only when not recognized; Rambam only stranger; still difficult why believed here on others; rule like Rashbag except Bechor penalty; Demai stranger cases; suspected believed on others except collusion; not like Raah; practical: suspected not worse than Demai; Taz disputes Rama d'oraisa on cheese purchase — Meshoreret HaBayit refutes; siman 2 slaughter when says expert so-and-so; siman 86 eggs seller himself; siman 118 thigh d'oraisa reasoning; habitual sinner for appetite not before blind; suspected thinks permitted — not believed d'oraisa; Perishah wrong on self-eating slaughterer — siman 2 refutes.`,
    '20#_': `Suspected. Shach: habitual sinner for that matter not others — unlike Bach. Taz: Beit Yosef brought Rambam suspected and gentile believed on others' tithes — no sin without self-benefit; disputes — we establish suspected not judged or testifies; so rule in practice.`,
  },
  'yad-avraham': {
    '1#_': `(siman 119 seif 8) One suspected regarding a severe prohibition is suspected regarding a lighter one. In Rashba responsum siman 64 — one suspected regarding the severe is not suspected regarding the lighter, as a habitual sinner regarding circumcision is not a habitual sinner regarding slaughter. One must say his intent is as Beit Yosef brought in Taz — only regarding food matters do we say suspected for severe is suspected for light, but not for two categories; or as Taz in the name of the Perishah — only when he has benefit from it, but without benefit not; and what Shach wrote on the Perishah requires study. See Dagul MeRevavah who wrote on Shach that there is no proof from this alone that even though it is severe in karet penalty he still has time all his life, unlike other prohibitions where if he transgressed again there is no remedy. This is according to what is written in the gloss below at the beginning of Laws of Circumcision that every day he transgresses a karet prohibition and therefore he is not remedied for the past by circumcising afterward.`,
  },
  'yad-ephraim': {
    '1#_': `Matters where there is concern of prohibition. Baer Heitev; see Peri Megadim part 2 siman 116: one about whom rumor spread that he had several jugs of stam yeinam to sell to Jews while presumed kosher, and he says the stam yeinam in his hand he already sold to a gentile and it was kosher, and the gentiles contradict him and doubt fell on other wines he brought whether he is believed they were made kosher — ruled that since he was compelled by community decree and judges to clarify to whom he sold his yayin nesech and he says to gentiles in such-and-such village they seized and confessed on their word — we presume him a liar and do not rely on him until he swears a severe oath as the judge sees fit that he sold to those gentiles and all wines he brought into the community were made kosher per our Torah law; if he does not swear he remains suspected and the wine he brought is presumed stam yeinam; responsa Pri Chadash Yoreh Deah siman 14 on reliability of those suspected in prohibitions and many distinctions regarding habitual sinners for appetite in slaughter or other prohibitions.`,
    '2#_': `Regarding other matters. Baer Heitev; responsa Masat Moshe Yoreh Deah siman 104: one who practices forbidding lighting his pipe from smoke called titun from a milk candle — forbidden to tell one who practices leniency to light his pipe from that candle unless he specifies not to light from a milk candle, since for himself he practices prohibition; there he inclined to permit one who practices leniency to light a milk candle to mislead one who practices prohibition by saying it is a wax candle, citing Maharalbach responsum 121 and Peri Chadash Orach Chaim 496 on customs of Ashkenaz; also proof from beginning of chapter HaIshah Shalom; Shach siman 119 brought Maharalbach; for practice it appears certainly forbidden to mislead with false speech; the proof can be rejected; I shortened.`,
    '3#_': `Even under oath. Baer Heitev; responsa Pri Chadash Yoreh Deah siman 16: Jews who went from other lands to purify yayin nesech and some did for themselves and some for other Jews as hired workers; they testified these drank yayin nesech daily and when asked why they said it is pikuach nefesh because in those lands water is bad and causes stomach illness and there is no other drink — ruled this is only appetite, for we do not testify they denied this mitzvah entirely, for perhaps if kosher wine were before them they would drink; and though not clear, since we did not see them drink when permitted wine was before them we establish the man on his presumption as Choshen Mishpat siman 34 if we saw him tying or untying on Shabbat, etc.; responsa Mayim Rabim 24–26: those suspected on stam yeinam are not believed to make others' wine in purity; responsa Devar Shmuel 61; also copied a responsum from another who is stringent; the author inclined to leniency by oath if they impose and threaten to speak truth if any incident occurred during wine work or their hand slipped from supervision entrusted to them and we rely on what they say under oath; see Bei Chayei and Chokhmat Adam siman 188.`,
    '4#_': `One who says. Responsa Tashbetz part 3 siman 83: cheese sent from the forced ones from those suspected of eating gentile cheeses — forbidden to eat, unlike Raah in Beit HaBehirah; he conceded to Ribash.`,
  },
};
